1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,467
What would it take to ask your AI to ship two pending orders and save the order list? Follow the request from your Windows PC into a practice virtual machine.

2
00:00:09,467 --> 00:00:16,333
If Claude Code or Codex can run commands on your PC, paste the repository address and this request.

3
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:24,600
Ask it to verify the download, connect the tool, and list your virtual machines. Approve the Windows prompt yourself.

4
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:32,533
In a web chat without access to your terminal, ask for one command at a time. Send back the result before moving on.

5
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:45,267
Your AI client talks to VAMP on the host. The order application and its clicks run inside the guest. This example uses Windows, Node.js 22 or later, and VirtualBox 7.

6
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:57,067
To install manually, open the GitHub release and expand Assets. Save the package and its checksum list in the same folder. The commands here use version zero point three point thirteen.

7
00:00:57,067 --> 00:01:05,600
Compare all sixty-four characters of the SHA-256 digest. Once they match, install the local package with npm.

8
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:13,067
Run the version command. For the file in this example, the output should be zero point three point thirteen.

9
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:23,400
For text and icon recognition, set up Python three point twelve and the appropriate device driver on the host. Run setup and allow time for the models to download.

10
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:33,133
With the Codex CLI installed on the same PC, add the server, then inspect its registration. Open a new Codex session for the first tool call.

11
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:42,067
Approve the host UAC prompt when the server connects. An empty list can be a successful response if you have not registered any VMs yet.

12
00:01:42,067 --> 00:01:51,200
To use the graphical manager, verify the VSIX from the same release and install it in VS Code. Reload the window and open VM Manager.

13
00:01:51,200 --> 00:02:00,733
Use an existing practice VM, or choose an ISO and create a new one. Open the guest desktop and load the separate Demo ERP Orders page.

14
00:02:00,733 --> 00:02:11,533
Start the guest recorder and perform the order update once. Stop the recording and save its Python file to the mounted VAMPRecords share. Guest Additions must mount the share.

15
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:22,333
In RPA Library, select the recording and choose Create. State what success looks like, check the guest resolution, and review the actions before allowing reuse.

16
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:34,667
Reset the practice data and open the original starting screen. Have the AI show the target and expected result before running the saved task. A separate CLI must point to the same RPA catalog.

17
00:02:34,667 --> 00:02:42,067
Two rows begin as pending. Updating each row changes it to shipped. The third row was already shipped.

18
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:51,133
Here is the project’s recorded VirtualBox guest at the export step. The resulting file gives us a way to check what the application actually saved.

19
00:02:51,133 --> 00:03:02,067
Open the new CSV. Check that the three expected order IDs appear once each and all three statuses say shipped. The companion guide includes a sample file for comparison.
